comments.body:
@participant6 I am assuming, that something networking wise is not working properly. The interesting part is, that I can not see the hello to docker and that is what is screwing with me. 

Thinking about it, that there might be no issue with the MTU. I am running the connections on 1500 MTU and it seems like it still screws up. 

My assumption is, that there is something wrong with the AWS Network which we have no influence on. Maybe the guys at Docker could probably look into it.

I think I found a workaround. As suspected, it was due to our network firewall.

@reporter I created a `no-inspection` exception for our docker host and everything worked fine afterwards. I think the problem is due to the use of Encrypted Client Hello (ECH).

@participant5 I think this also explains why you couldn't see the client hello as well, as the ECH would encrypt that package. I think you have a firewall sitting between the vehicles and the docker servers doing SSL inspections and blocking ECH by default.

@participant5 The SSL inspection might be taking place after the vehicle as well. I assume the vehicles are on a special network of some sort, and someone at the internet gateway level might be doing an SSL inspection to limit which destinations are allowed. 

Here's what I mean, if someone between the vehicles and the internet there's a firewall that want to block `example.com` then it needs to see the TLS Client Hello to understand which destinations you're connecting, otherwise all it sees is an IP address and not the host. The firewall would usually be able to see this information because the client hello is not encrypted, or if it's going to do deep package inspection, it would send its own certificate back and practically do a MITM (man in the middle) style package inspection. 

In our case, either a) firewall is blocking the encrypted client hello messages so that I can inspect which destinations are being reached, or b) there's a firewall firmware issue where it's failing to send its own certificate to conduct a deep package inspection. Our devices are configured to trust the root certificate authority of the firewall, so had it send the certificate properly, the connection would've continued.
